# Deployment configuration and secrets for the Raspberry Pi stack.
#
# Copy this to /opt/solax/.env ON THE PI and fill it in -- it never leaves that machine and is not
# committed (the repo's .gitignore covers "*.env"). deploy.sh does not copy it.
#
#   scp deploy/.env.example martin@192.168.2.7:/opt/solax/.env
#   ssh martin@192.168.2.7 'chmod 600 /opt/solax/.env && nano /opt/solax/.env'
#
# docker compose reads this file for the ${...} substitutions in docker-compose.yml, and for two of
# its own variables that control the stack shape rather than any one setting: COMPOSE_PROFILES
# (below) and COMPOSE_FILE (under Web UI). See deploy/README.md's "Running without Home Assistant"
# for the combinations this makes possible and their memory budgets.

# --- Image -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One package covers every platform (arm64 here, amd64 and Windows elsewhere) as a multi-platform
# manifest list, so this tag never needs an architecture in it -- docker picks the right image.
#
#   latest        newest build of main
#   1.0.0         a released version. NOTE: no "v", though the git tag is v1.0.0 -- the publish
#                 workflow strips the prefix. IMAGE_TAG=v1.0.0 fails with "manifest unknown".
#   sha-abc1234   one specific build, immutable -- what to pin to when rolling back
#
#   IMAGE_TAG=sha-abc1234 ./deploy/deploy.sh
#
# Only if a release is held up by one platform failing to build: the single-platform tags are
# published regardless, so IMAGE_TAG=sha-abc1234-linux-arm64 still gets this Pi running.
IMAGE_TAG=latest

# --- Site --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TZ=Europe/Prague
INVERTER_HOST=192.168.2.6
EV_CHARGER_HOST=192.168.2.10

# --- Home Assistant + MQTT broker (optional) ----------------------------------------------------
# Both are opt-in: the controller runs, and can be fully controlled from its own web UI below, with
# neither. Turning this on takes THREE settings that have to agree, not one:
#
#   1. COMPOSE_PROFILES=mosquitto,homeassistant   -- actually starts the two containers
#   2. HOMEASSISTANT_ENABLED=true                 -- the controller's own switch to publish MQTT
#   3. MQTT_USERNAME / MQTT_PASSWORD below         -- must match the broker's password file (README step 7)
#
# Leaving all three at their defaults (unset / false) is what makes a controller-only deployment
# possible: no COMPOSE_PROFILES means `docker compose up -d` starts nothing but solax-controller, and
# HOMEASSISTANT_ENABLED=false means it never tries to reach a broker that was never started.
#
# **Upgrading from a deployment that predates compose profiles?** Set all three below to keep running
# exactly what you had before -- without them, the next `docker compose up -d` starts the controller
# alone and Home Assistant and the broker simply stop.
#COMPOSE_PROFILES=mosquitto,homeassistant
#HOMEASSISTANT_ENABLED=true

# Must match the credentials in the broker's password file. Create that file with:
#   docker run --rm -v /opt/solax/mosquitto/config:/mosquitto/config eclipse-mosquitto:2 \
#     mosquitto_passwd -c -b /mosquitto/config/passwd solax '<password>'
MQTT_USERNAME=solax
MQTT_PASSWORD=

# --- Self-hosted web UI (optional) ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The controller's own dashboard, alternative to Home Assistant or alongside it -- see the root
# README's "Self-hosted web UI" section for what it shows and controls. Off by default, like Home
# Assistant. Turning it on needs the port actually published, which -- for the same "off means no
# socket at all" reason HOMEASSISTANT_ENABLED is a separate switch from COMPOSE_PROFILES -- is a
# second file rather than a line that always applies:
#
#   COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.web.yml
#
#WEB_ENABLED=true
#COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.web.yml
#WEB_PORT=8080
#WEB_REQUIRE_AUTHENTICATION=true

# Secret -- the hash of the single shared password, never the password itself. Generate it with the
# image itself (no configuration or listening socket involved; prints the hash and exits):
#
#   docker run --rm ghcr.io/mpospisil/solax-controller:latest hash-password '<your password>'
#
# Required whenever WEB_ENABLED=true and WEB_REQUIRE_AUTHENTICATION is left at its default true --
# the controller refuses to start otherwise rather than silently serving an unprotected UI.
#WEB_PASSWORD_HASH=

# --- Solcast (optional) ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without these the worker logs a warning and skips forecast refreshes; everything else still runs.
SOLCAST_API_KEY=
SOLCAST_RESOURCE_ID=

# --- Hardware-writing features -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Left at the shipped defaults deliberately: deploying must not start writing to your inverter or
# charger. The service always boots with charge control in mode Off and takes control only when you
# select a mode (Home Assistant or the web UI), so there is no "enable" flag for it -- only a dry
# run. Change these only after verifying the register addresses against your own device (see README).
#CHARGE_CONTROL_DRY_RUN=false
#BATTERY_HOLD_ENABLED=false
#BATTERY_HOLD_DRY_RUN=true

# --- Memory limits -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sized for a 1 GB Pi 3 B; the three together leave roughly 170 MB for the OS and page cache. Only
# relevant to the services actually started (see COMPOSE_PROFILES above) -- a controller-only
# deployment needs none of this. Requires cgroup memory accounting to be enabled in
# /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt -- see README.
#HA_MEM_LIMIT=600m
#CONTROLLER_MEM_LIMIT=200m
#MOSQUITTO_MEM_LIMIT=48m
